[# Section 2 — the CX data language.
   Authored 2026-05-22. Anchors normative depth in
   spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md, spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md,
   spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf, spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md,
   spec/03-approved/core/schema.md, spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md.
   File extension: .cxd (data-only source, per d-2026-05-22-05). #]

[section id=data group='Ring 0 — Data' n=2 title='The data language'

  [intro
    """CX's data language is what you write in a `.cx` or `.cxd` file when
       you mean "this is data." It is also what every CX code expression
       evaluates to — programs and values share one shape. This section is
       the working reference for the data surface; the normative grammar
       is in [`spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf`](../../spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf)
       and the value model is in
       [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md)."""]

  [child id=lexical n=2.1 title='Lexical structure'
    [intro
      """Every CX source file is UTF-8 text. The lexer is small: identifiers,
         scalar literals, brackets (`[`, `]`), parentheses (used for sequences
         and argument lists), curly braces (used for map literals), sigils,
         strings, and comments. The full lexical rules are in
         [`spec/03-approved/formal/lexicon.ebnf`](../../spec/03-approved/formal/lexicon.ebnf)."""]

    [child id=identifiers n=2.1.1 title='Identifiers'
      [body
        """An identifier starts with an ASCII letter or underscore and
           continues with letters, digits, underscores, and hyphens. Hyphens
           are valid identifier characters (CX leans on the kebab-case
           convention used by Lisp, Clojure, and Scheme). The reserved tokens
           `true`, `false`, `null`, and the directive names (starting with
           `?`) are excluded. See [[reserved-tokens]] for the complete
           reserved-name list."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[user-profile first-name='Ada' last-name='Lovelace']"""]
      [body
        """Names of CX elements are stored as Unicode code-point sequences;
           a single canonical byte form is used at the wire level (see
           [[canonical]]). Unicode normalization is NFC — composed form is
           the canonical shape."""]]

    [child id=comments n=2.1.2 title='Comments (line and block)'
      [body
        """CX has two comment forms: a `#` line comment that runs to
           end-of-line, and a `[; … ]` block comment that spans any number of
           lines and is allowed anywhere whitespace is allowed. The block
           comment is a real lexical element, not a hack on top of a string
           — the parser drops it before AST construction. Comments are
           preserved by the lossless canonicalizer (`cx fmt`) and stripped
           by the strict canonicalizer (`cx canonical`); see [[canonical]]."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[; A single-line note. ]
           [config
             [; A block comment can nest inside an element
                and span lines. ]
             [host 'localhost']]"""]
      [note
        """Older CX dialects used `[-` `-]` for block comments. CX currently uses
           the `[; … ]` form only; `[# … #]` is raw text / CDATA, not a
           comment. See [[migration]] for the rename."""]]

    [child id=whitespace n=2.1.3 title='Whitespace'
      [body
        """Whitespace separates tokens; otherwise it is not significant.
           Indentation does not change meaning. CX is bracket-structured, so
           you can compact a document to one line, or pretty-print it across
           many — the canonical form (see [[cx-canonical]]) is unique and
           hash-stable either way."""]
      [body
        """Inside element bodies, whitespace separates body items — it is
           structure, not content. The content itself is verbatim: quoted
           and triple-quoted strings preserve their internal whitespace
           exactly as authored. There is no whitespace-collapse pass and
           no mode attribute to switch one on (see [[xml-space]])."""]]]

  [child id=elements n=2.2 title='Elements'
    [intro
      """An **element** is the basic CX data structure: a name, zero or more
         attributes, and a body. Every bracketed form is an element. The
         normative production is
         [`spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf`](../../spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf)
         `[51] Element`."""]

    [child id=names n=2.2.1 title='Element names'
      [body
        """The first token inside `[` is the element name. It is an
           identifier (or a string literal, if you need spaces, but that is
           unusual in idiomatic CX). Names starting with `?` denote
           directives (CX code, not data) — see [[code]]."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[server]                       [# empty element                 #]
           [server host='a.com']          [# attributes only               #]
           [server [host 'a.com']]        [# nested elements as body       #]"""]]

    [child id=attributes n=2.2.2 title='Attributes'
      [body
        """Attributes are key-value pairs in the form `name=value`. The key
           is an identifier; the value is a bare scalar, a quoted string,
           a numeric literal, a boolean literal, a date, or `null`. Multiple
           attributes are space-separated within the element header."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[user id=42 email='ada@example.com' active=true]"""]
      [note
        """A bare `:name` in body position is NOT attribute syntax — it is
           an **atom literal** (`:ok`, `:not-found`; see [[type]]). The
           labeled-slot directive syntax that once used `:name` is retired:
           directive clauses are explicit child elements (`[then …]`,
           `[yield …]`). On a plain data element you always write
           `name=value`."""]
      [body
        """Attribute order is preserved by the parser **and is part of the
           canonical identity** — order carries meaning in CX, so
           re-ordering attributes produces a different document with a
           different SHA-256. Nothing in canonicalization sorts attributes
           (see [[canonical]]). One sigil-prefixed attribute
           shortcut exists: `#name` (the id sigil, see [[id]]). The
           anchor sigil `&name` and the merge sigil `*name` are also
           attribute-level — see [[anchor]] and [[merge]]."""]]

    [child id=bodies n=2.2.3 title='Body content'
      [body
        """The body of an element is everything between the attribute list
           and the closing `]`. A body may be empty, a single scalar, a
           sequence of scalars and elements (mixed content / prose — see
           [[prose]]), or a sequence of just elements."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[empty]
           [scalar 42]
           [prose 'Some ' [em 'mixed'] ' content.']
           [list [item 1] [item 2] [item 3]]"""]
      [body
        """A bare scalar body is auto-typed per the rules in
           [`spec/03-approved/core/ast.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/ast.md)
           §Scalar — `42` becomes an int, `3.14` a float, `2026-05-22` a
           date, etc. To force a type, glue an ascription to the name:
           `[port::u16 8080]`."""]]]

  [child id=scalars n=2.3 title='Scalar kinds'
    [intro
      """CX has **nine** scalar kinds per
         [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md)
         §2: string, int, float, bool, date, datetime, bytes, null, and
         atom. Additional type names refine storage and precision on top
         of those kinds — `decimal`, `bigint`, the sized integer / float
         widths, `duration`, `period`, `instant`, `secret`. The
         wire encoding for each kind is in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
         §3. Each kind can be introduced literally (e.g. `42` for int) or
         forced with a glued type ascription (`count::int`, `port::u32`)."""]

    [child id=strings n=2.3.1 title='Strings — single, double, triple-quoted'
      [body
        """Strings come in three forms. Single-quoted (`'…'`) and
           double-quoted (`"…"`) work the same way and support escapes.
           Triple-quoted (`'''…'''` or `\"\"\"…\"\"\"`) strings span lines,
           preserve internal whitespace, and need no escaping for the other
           quote characters."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[greeting 'Hello, world']
           [path "C:\\Users\\Ada"]
           [readme \"\"\"
             This is a
             multi-line string
             with 'embedded' quotes.
           \"\"\"]"""]
      [body
        """Triple-quoted strings are the default for any body text longer
           than one line. They are the building block for the prose form
           (see [[prose]]) and for the `[?modify]` action `:set` values
           when those values are documents in their own right."""]
      [body
        """The canonical form (`cx canonical`) emits single-quoted strings for
           single-line content and triple-double-quoted strings for
           multi-line content. Escapes are minimised to what the parser
           strictly requires."""]]

    [child id=numbers n=2.3.2 title='Numbers — int, float, sized, hex'
      [body
        """Integer literals are plain digits, optionally signed:
           `42`, `-17`, `0`. Float literals contain a decimal point or
           exponent: `3.14`, `1e9`, `-2.5e-3`. Hex literals are written
           `0x…`. The default int is a 64-bit signed integer; the default
           float is IEEE-754 double. Sized variants are available via
           `::T` ascriptions when binding to host languages where the size
           matters; see [[sized-types]]."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[answer 42]
           [pi 3.14159]
           [big 1e18]
           [color 0xff8800]
           [port::u16 8080]
           [pixel-density::f32 1.5]"""]
      [body
        """`nan`, `inf`, and `-inf` are **not** float literals — those
           tokens parse as ordinary strings, and CX floats are
           finite-only in canonical form (a NaN or infinity has no
           canonical bytes). Non-finite values can only arrive across
           the FFI boundary; `[$math:is-nan]` exists for exactly that
           boundary work. `-0.0` and `+0.0` compare equal under `=` but
           produce different canonical bytes."""]
      [body
        """An integer **literal** that exceeds the 64-bit range
           auto-promotes to `bigint` — `[big 9223372036854775808]`
           parses fine. Only an explicit ascription pins the width and
           makes overflow an error: `[big::int 9223372036854775808]`
           refuses. Runtime **arithmetic** is checked, not promoted:
           adding `1` to `9223372036854775807` (max i64) raises
           `CXER3000` (E_MATH_OVERFLOW)."""]]

    [child id=booleans n=2.3.3 title='Booleans'
      [body
        """`true` and `false`. They are reserved tokens; the identifier
           `true` always parses as the boolean true value. Use sigil
           forms (see [[bool]]) for compact attribute syntax."""]]

    [child id=dates n=2.3.4 title='Dates and datetimes'
      [body
        """Dates are ISO-8601 calendar dates: `2026-05-22`. Datetimes are
           ISO-8601 with time and optional zone offset:
           `2026-05-22T14:30:00Z` or `2026-05-22T14:30:00-04:00`. Both are
           first-class scalars — they round-trip across the lossless
           projections and have stable canonical byte encodings. See
           [[datetime-detail]] for timezone, precision, and leap-second
           semantics."""]]

    [child id='null' n=2.3.5 title='Null'
      [body
        """`null` is the absent-value scalar. Distinct from the empty
           sequence `()`. `null` is a value; `()` is the absence of any
           value — `null` is a single-item sequence containing the null
           scalar, `()` is an empty sequence, and presence tests tell
           them apart. Under the effective-boolean-value rule
           ([`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md)
           §6) **both are falsy**: a singleton `null` evaluates to
           false, and `()` evaluates to false."""]]

    [child id=bytes n=2.3.6 title='Bytes — raw binary scalars'
      [body
        """`:bytes` is the eighth scalar kind: a length-prefixed byte
           sequence with no character semantics. Use it for image data,
           hashes, encrypted payloads, or any opaque binary value."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[hash::bytes 0x3a7bd3e2360a3d29eea436fcfb7e44c735d117c42d1c1835420b6b9942dd4f1b]
           [magic::bytes 0x89504e470d0a1a0a]"""]
      [body
        """A bytes value is a `0x`-prefixed hex literal (even digit count)
           under a `::bytes` ascription. The canonical form emits the hex
           string with the `0x` prefix; the data-bin wire format uses raw
           length-prefixed bytes (no encoding overhead). The conversion
           contract maps bytes to base64 / tagged forms per lane — see
           [[bytes-wire]] in [[surfaces]] for the per-surface table."""]]

    [child id=sized-types n=2.3.7 title='Sized type tags'
      [body
        """When binding CX to host languages where integer size matters
           (Rust `i32` vs `i64`, Go `uint8` vs `int`, C `int16_t`),
           a sized ascription is glued to the name with `::`. The full
           set:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Signed integers:** `::i8`, `::i16`, `::i32`, `::i64`."""]
        [item """**Unsigned integers:** `::u8`, `::u16`, `::u32`, `::u64`."""]
        [item """**Floats:** `::f16`, `::f32`, `::f64`."""]
        [item """**Default-int:** `::int` — alias for `::i64`."""]
        [item """**Default-float:** `::float` — alias for `::f64`."""]]
      [body
        """A literal without an ascription is auto-typed as int (for whole
           numbers) or float (for numbers with `.` or `e`). To force a
           sized variant: `[port::u16 8080]`, `[pixel-density::f32 1.5]`.
           Sized variants flow through ast-bin and data-bin with their
           type identifier preserved; bindings translate to the host's
           native numeric type."""]
      [body
        """Overflow on a sized scalar (writing `300` into `::u8`, or
           `2147483648` into `::i32`) is refused at parse time with
           `CXERLEX-RANGE` — the error names the value and the width
           it does not fit."""]]]

  [child id=collections n=2.4 title='Collection literals'
    [intro
      """CX has three collection literal forms: **sequence**, **array**,
         and **map**. The distinction is load-bearing for function
         signatures — see
         [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md)
         §2 and [[container-atom]] below."""]

    [child id=seq n=2.4.1 title='Sequence literals (a, b, c)'
      [body
        """A comma-separated parenthesized list is a sequence: `(1, 2, 3)`.
           Sequences are flat — concatenating sequences never produces a
           sequence-of-sequences (the XQuery sequence-flat rule, see
           [[seq-flat]])."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[ports (80, 443, 8080)]"""]
      [body
        """A sequence with one element is equivalent to that single
           element: `(42)` ≡ `42`. The empty sequence is `()` — distinct
           from `null` (see [[null]])."""]]

    [child id=arr n=2.4.2 title='Array literals [a, b, c]'
      [body
        """A bracketed comma-separated list (distinguished from an element
           by the absence of a name and the presence of commas) is an
           array. Arrays preserve nesting — an array of arrays stays
           shaped that way."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[matrix [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]]"""]
      [body
        """Array indexing is **1-based**, like CXPath positional
           predicates (`$doc/item[1]` selects the first item) — per
           [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md)
           §2.5. Out-of-range access yields the empty sequence rather
           than an error. There is no negative-index shorthand."""]]

    [child id=map n=2.4.3 title='Map literals {k: v}'
      [body
        """Curly braces wrap a map literal: keys are atomic scalars, values
           are **one expression-shaped item each** — a scalar, a quoted
           string, a nested collection, or an entity reference. Multi-word
           prose must be quoted (`{note: 'two words'}`); an unquoted run
           is a loud parse error, never silently absorbed. Keys must be
           unique; a duplicate key raises
           `W014` (`CXERMAP-DUPKEY`). Insertion order is preserved for round-trip
           but not for canonical identity — two maps with the same
           key-value pairs hash to the same canonical bytes regardless of
           insertion order."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[env {host: 'a.com', port: 8080, tls: true}]"""]
      [body
        """Entries separate by comma **or plain whitespace/newlines** — the
           same rule arrays follow (`[1 2 3]` ≡ `[1, 2, 3]`):"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[env {host: 'a.com' port: 8080
                 tls: true}]"""]
      [body
        """The map key type is `ScalarKey` per cxdm §2: bool, int, float,
           string, date, datetime, or bytes. `null` is not a valid key
           (`null = null` would not satisfy the uniqueness invariant
           under value-comparison semantics). Mixed-type keys are
           permitted; comparison is by canonical bytes of each key."""]
      [body
        """The `::` annotation takes one meaning per position. Glued to the
           **key** it types the key (`{1.10::decimal: x}` — a decimal key,
           scale-distinct from `{1.1::decimal: x}`). In the **value slot**
           it types the value, coercion-checked, spelled either way:
           type-first (`{age: ::int 30}`) or postfix
           (`{price: 19.99::decimal}`) — one canonical image either way
           (`{price: soon::decimal}` is a loud `CXER0290`, and an unknown
           tag like `5::bogus` is a loud `CXER0107` — never silent text).
           Standing **alone after the colon** it *declares* the field:
           typed, value ABSENT — not null:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[props {order: ::string, compact: ::bool, tags: ::string[]}]"""]
      [body
        """A declared entry's kind draws from the same kind vocabulary bind
           patterns test (`int`, `string`, `element`, `array`, `path`, …,
           plus `[]` for array kinds). Reading a declared entry's value
           refuses — absence is not null — and only the `cx` and
           round-trip-XML lanes can carry a declaration; every lossy
           target (JSON, YAML, CSV, …) refuses the document loudly."""]]

    [child id=seq-flat n=2.4.4 title='The sequence-flat principle'
      [body
        """**Every value in CX is a sequence; sequences do not nest at the
           sequence boundary.** This is XQuery's sequence-flat rule
           (cxdm §1) — the property that makes the expression family
           compose."""]
      [list
        [item """`//user` returns a sequence whether the document has 0,
                1, or N users — consumers iterate uniformly."""]
        [item """Concatenation is total and associative:
                `concat(a, b, c)` never raises "can't concatenate
                scalar to list"."""]
        [item """A directive's return value is "what it evaluates to";
                multiple emits naturally concatenate."""]]
      [body
        """What sequence-flat is NOT: a list-of-lists model. Concatenating
           two sequences yields one flat sequence, never a
           sequence-containing-two-sub-sequences. To preserve nesting use
           **arrays** ([[arr]]) — arrays are container Items, not
           sequence boundaries."""]]

    [child id=container-atom n=2.4.5 title='Container vs atom distinction'
      [body
        """The value model draws a load-bearing distinction between **atom**
           Items (Node, Scalar) and **container** Items (Array, Map,
           Sequence-as-Item) per
           [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md)
           §2.1:"""]
      [list
        [item """**Atoms** are leaf values from the value-model perspective.
                A Scalar `42` is a single Item; a Node is a single Item even
                though its tree contents are richly structured."""]
        [item """**Containers** hold further Items by composition at the
                value level. `[1, 2, 3]` is one Array Item that holds three
                Scalar Items. `{name: 'a'}` is one Map Item that holds one
                (key, value) pair."""]]
      [body
        """This governs which operations apply where, and the rule is a
           **read/construct split**. *Readers* — `[$count]`, `[$length]`,
           `[$empty]`, `[$exists]`, `[$first]`, `[$last]`, `[$nth]`,
           `[$head]`, `[$tail]`, `[$distinct]` — destructure **any**
           collection kind and answer over its items:
           `[$count [1, 2, 3]]` is `3` on an Array exactly as it is on a
           Sequence. Orderedness is a property of the value, not a wrapper
           the reader must peel first. *Constructors and combiners* keep
           the strict container typing: `[$concat [1, 2] [3]]` is a type
           error, because building a Sequence out of Arrays would erase
           the very distinction the container kinds carry. Map values are
           read by key — `$m.name` on `{name: 'a'}` yields `'a'`."""]
      [body
        """The practical impact: readers never make you convert just to
           ask a question, so there is no silent wrong answer of a
           collection counting as one scalar — while anything that
           *builds* a value type-checks its containers strictly, keeping
           construction intent visible to readers and tools."""]]]

  [child id=sigils n=2.5 title='Sigils'
    [intro
      """Sigils are one-character prefixes that change how a token is
         interpreted. CX has three element-level sigils — `&` (anchor),
         `*` (merge), `#` (id) — plus `@` (reference to a declared id,
         in attribute-value position or in a body `[ref @name]`), and
         `:`, which serves the glued `::T` type ascription and the
         `:name` atom literal."""]

    [child id=anchor n=2.5.1 title='The & anchor sigil'
      [body
        """`&name` declares a reusable shape anchor on an element. Other
           elements can refer to it later in the same document via the
           merge sigil `*name`. Anchors are document-scoped — they
           resolve within the same document and do not cross include
           boundaries (per [[include-capabilities]])."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[defaults &server-base host='localhost' tls=true]
           [service-a *server-base port=8001]
           [service-b *server-base port=8002]"""]
      [body
        """A duplicate `&name` declaration is **not** an error — the first
           definition in document order wins, deterministically. The
           lossless canonicalizer preserves anchors and aliases; the strict
           canonicalizer expands them and the canonical bytes are the
           expanded form. The lossy projections (`--json` / `--yaml` /
           `--csv` / …) and a program's `$doc` read that same expanded
           form — the semantic document — while default CX and XML
           output keep the authored sharing."""]]

    [child id=merge n=2.5.2 title='The * merge sigil'
      [body
        """`*name` expands the anchor's attributes and body into the
           current element. Local attributes override merged ones, so you
           can specialize. A cyclic `*` reference has no finite expanded
           form, so canonicalization refuses the document outright (a
           hard error naming the cycle, without a `CXER` code)."""]]

    [child id=id n=2.5.3 title='The # id sigil'
      [body
        """`#name` declares an identity on the element; `@name`
           references it. Identities are document-unique.
           Identities differ from anchors: anchors are for shape reuse
           (data-level), identities are for cross-references (semantic
           link). Full semantics in
           [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md) §4."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[user #ada email='ada@example.com']
           [post author-id=@ada 'First post']"""]
      [body
        """An `@name` reference (as in `author-id=@ada` above) resolves to
           the element bearing `#ada`. The reference does not embed; it
           remains a pointer. In body position the reference form is
           `[ref @ada]`. Cross-format identity: only CX and XML carry
           the ID/IDREF mechanism losslessly; the JSON/YAML/TOML/MD
           lanes flatten it to an ordinary `id` field plus a `$ref`
           envelope, one-way. See [[identity]]."""]]

    [child id=type n=2.5.4 title='The : type sigil'
      [body
        """`:` is reserved for two surface roles. Glued to a name as `::T`
           (`[port::u16 8080]`) it is the type-ascription sigil — the
           doubled form is the only ascription spelling; a spaced `:name`
           in body position is never a type. In **atom position** (`:ok`,
           `:not-found`) it introduces a symbolic atom literal. The
           parser disambiguates by context. The `:` sigil is no
           longer used to introduce labeled directive slots — every
           clause is now an explicit child element (`[then …]`,
           `[else …]`, `[yield …]`, `[where …]`)."""]
      [body
        """Type ascriptions are explicit; the nine scalar kinds (see
           [[scalars]]) plus sized variants (see [[sized-types]]) all use
           the glued `::T` form when written explicitly. The code
           language's value-kind test uses the same vocabulary."""]]

    [child id=bool n=2.5.5 title='Boolean attributes'
      [body
        """Booleans are ordinary attributes: `active=true`,
           `banned=false`. The bare tokens `true` and `false` auto-type
           as bool (see [[scalars]]); there is no shorthand sigil form —
           an attribute is always spelled `name=value`."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[user email='ada@example.com' active=true banned=false]"""]]]

  [child id=prose n=2.6 title='Mixed content and prose'
    [intro
      """Mixed content — text interleaved with elements — is first-class
         CX, not a separate "string mode". The body of any element may
         alternate freely between scalars and child elements. This makes
         Markdown projection lossless: a paragraph with inline code is
         stored as a `[p]` element containing a text run, a `[code]`
         element, and more text."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[para 'The function ' [code 'parse'] ' returns a '
         [link to='spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md' 'Document'] ' value.']"""]

    [child id=entity-refs n=2.6.1 title='Entity references'
      [body
        """CX text accepts the five XML/HTML entity references: `&amp;`,
           `&lt;`, `&gt;`, `&quot;`, `&apos;`. They are **not decoded**
           — an entity reference parses to an entity-reference node and
           is re-emitted verbatim, byte-for-byte, in both canonical
           forms. `[t 'a &amp; b']` canonicalizes to exactly that."""]
      [body
        """Numeric character references (`&#65;`, `&#x41;`) are carried
           the same verbatim way. There is no textual expansion pass at
           all — which is why recursive-expansion attacks (the XML
           "billion laughs") have nothing to attack (see [[limits]]).
           External entity resolution (the XML
           `<!ENTITY foo SYSTEM "…"> &foo;` pattern) does not exist in
           CX; DTD content rides through as verbatim prolog data (see
           [[surfaces]])."""]]

    [child id=xml-space n=2.6.2 title='Whitespace preservation (xml:space)'
      [body
        """Quoted body text is **verbatim** — CX has no
           whitespace-collapse pass, so there is nothing for a mode
           attribute to switch. Internal runs of spaces and newlines
           inside a quoted or triple-quoted string survive parse,
           canonicalization, and round-trip exactly as authored."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[poem
           '  Roses are red,
             Violets are blue,
              Whitespace preserved,
               just for you.']"""]
      [body
        """The XML-interop attribute `xml:space` is accepted and carried
           as an **inert pass-through attribute** — it round-trips
           through XML identically and rides through JSON/YAML/TOML as a
           regular attribute, but CX itself attaches no behavior to
           it."""]]

    [child id=cdata n=2.6.3 title='CDATA sections'
      [body
        """XML CDATA blocks (`<![CDATA[...]]>`) project to CX raw-text
           nodes (`[#…#]`) on import — the contents are opaque bytes,
           untouched by canonicalization. CX itself has no separate
           CDATA syntax: raw text carries opaque payloads, and
           triple-quoted strings carry verbatim string content."""]]]

  [child id=tables n=2.7 title='Typed columnar tables'
    [intro
      """For analytical workloads CX has a typed columnar table form: a
         glued `[table[…]]` clause on the element head declares the
         columns once, and the body is the cells. The physical wire
         format is CXCol (see [[cxcol]]) and the normative table API is
         in [`spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/table-api.md)."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[orders [table[id::int customer amount::float ts::datetime]]
           1 Ada   42.50 2026-05-22T10:00:00Z
           2 Grace 17.25 2026-05-22T10:15:00Z
           3 Linus 99.00 2026-05-22T11:30:00Z
         ]"""]
    [body
      """**Column spec:** each column is a name with an optional glued
         `::T` type (`id::int`); an untyped column defaults to string
         (`customer` above). Types use the full scalar vocabulary
         (`::int`, `::float`, `::bool`, `::date`, `::datetime`,
         `::bytes`, plus sized variants `::i32`/`::u64`/`::f32` etc.).
         There is no nullable marker in the header — a `null` cell is
         legal in any typed column, and the binary wire carries such
         columns under a nullable-column wrapper
         ([`spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/data-bin.md)
         §3.10.5)."""]
    [body
      """**Rows are count-driven:** whitespace separates both cells and
         rows, and every N consecutive cells form one row, where N is
         the declared column count. A cell that does not satisfy its
         column type, or a total cell count that is not a multiple of
         N, is a parse error."""]
    [body
      """**Dictionary encoding:** on the binary wire, a column is
         dictionary-encoded exactly when the dictionary actually saves
         bytes — the writer evaluates the trade per column. There is no
         cardinality threshold to configure and no per-column attribute;
         the choice is automatic and canonical."""]
    [body
      """**Streaming:** oversized tables are emitted as chunked tables —
         column specs once, then length-prefixed row groups (canonical
         chunk size 2²⁰ rows per group). The length prefixes are the
         skip-ahead mechanism; there are no per-chunk statistics. See
         [[cxcol]] for the native streaming format and
         [[parquet-bridge]] for the Parquet round-trip."""]
    [body
      """**Arrow / Parquet bridges:** tables convert to Apache Arrow record
         batches (zero-copy via the C Data Interface) and to Parquet
         files. The bridges live on the analytics-bridge surface — see
         [[arrow-bridge]] and [[parquet-bridge]]."""]]

  [child id=schema n=2.8 title='Schema language (.cxs)'
    [intro
      """A `.cxs` file is a CX schema: an element-shape declaration that
         the validator can enforce. Schemas are CX documents themselves —
         no separate grammar to learn. Full spec in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/schema.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/schema.md)."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[; users.cxs ]
         [schema of=user]
         [user
           [attr id::int [req]]
           [attr email::string [req]]
           [attr active::bool [default true]]]"""]

    [child id=schema-modes n=2.8.1 title='Open / strict / closed modes'
      [body
        """Schemas declare one of three validation modes:"""]
      [list
        [item """**open** — the schema's declarations are required; extra
                attributes / extra child elements not declared are
                permitted silently, with no diagnostic. Use this for
                forward-compatible evolution."""]
        [item """**strict** — declarations are required; extras are
                reported as **warnings** (`S001` for unknown elements,
                `S012` for unknown attributes). Use for schema
                authoring — find missing declarations without failing
                the build."""]
        [item """**closed** — declarations are required; extras are
                **errors** (the same `S001` / `S012`, at error
                severity). Use for security-critical surfaces
                where the schema is the authoritative contract."""]]
      [body
        """The default mode is open. Override per-schema with
           `[schema of=… mode=strict]` — mode is per-schema, not
           per-element. See
           [`spec/03-approved/core/schema.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/schema.md)
           §9 for the full normative behavior."""]]

    [child id=schema-constraints n=2.8.2 title='Constraint vocabulary'
      [body
        """Beyond type-tag matching, schemas support a constraint
           vocabulary on attributes and element bodies:"""]
      [list
        [item """`[pattern S]` — string value matches the RE2 regex"""]
        [item """`[range M N]` / `[min M] [max N]` — numeric bounds"""]
        [item """`[len M N]` / `[min-length M] [max-length N]` — string/bytes length"""]
        [item """`[enum V ...]` — value is one of the listed values"""]
        [item """`[req]` / `[opt]` — presence policy"""]
        [item """`[card "M..N"]` — child element cardinality range"""]
        [item """`[default V]` — inserted when absent"""]]
      [body
        """Constraint violations produce `S`-prefixed diagnostics
           (`S002` missing required attribute, `S006` range violation,
           …) with the failing constraint identified in the message.
           Full vocabulary + error-code registry:
           [`spec/03-approved/core/schema.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/schema.md)
           §7 + §12."""]]

    [child id=schema-defaults n=2.8.3 title='Defaults and inheritance'
      [body
        """An attribute with a `[default V]` clause is materialized only
           when default application is requested — `--apply-defaults` on
           the CLI, or the `cx_validate_apply_defaults` ABI entry point,
           which returns a **modified copy**; plain validation never
           changes the document. A default whose literal type does not
           match the declared type fails at schema-load time, so schema
           typos surface immediately. Schemas can compose via
           `[?cx include]` (see [[includes]]); imported schemas'
           declarations layer over the importing schema's."""]]

    [child id=schema-binding n=2.8.4 title='Binding a schema to a document'
      [body
        """There is **one** way to associate a `.cxs` schema with a
           document today: the explicit CLI flag —
           `cx validate doc.cx --schema=users.cxs`. There is no
           sibling-file convention and no implicit lookup. The
           in-document `[?cx schema=…]` directive is defined by
           [`spec/03-approved/core/schema.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/schema.md)
           §13 and parses cleanly, but the shipped validator does not
           consume it — it requires the flag."""]
      [body
        """Schema-driven binary encoding (see [[data-bin]]) uses the
           schema's declarations to omit per-value type tags from the
           wire form — producing wire sizes comparable to
           Protobuf or Avro. Encoding is decoupled from validation and
           is its own explicit opt-in."""]]]

  [child id=includes n=2.9 title='Include resolver'
    [intro
      """`[?cx include]` resolves a path to another CX document and
         splices its contents in place. Resolution is opt-in: with no
         include root supplied, the directive is left in place
         unresolved — and because strict canonicalization strips
         directives, an unresolved include **silently vanishes** from
         `cx canonical` / `cx hash` output. Resolve before you hash.
         The refusal catalog (`cx-err:E901`–`E905`) is in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md)."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[config
           [?cx include=base.cx]
           [override port=9090]]"""]

    [child id=include-paths n=2.9.1 title='Path resolution'
      [body
        """Include paths are resolved relative to the **including
           document's directory**, not the working directory — and the
           whole resolution is **root-bounded**: the embedding supplies
           an absolute include root, and every resolved include must lie
           under it. Everything that could reach outside the root is
           refused, each with its own error: an absolute path
           (`cx-err:E901`), a relative path that traverses out of the
           root (`cx-err:E902`), and any URL scheme — `https:` included
           (`cx-err:E903`). An include is a local, root-bounded file
           read by construction; there is no search path and no
           environment variable — one root, one resolution rule."""]]

    [child id=include-cycles n=2.9.2 title='Cycle detection'
      [body
        """An include cycle (A includes B which includes A) is refused
           with `cx-err:E904` at the second visit, and the error names
           the whole chain (`A → B → A`). The resolver tracks include
           ancestors per document; sibling includes that happen to
           reference the same file are fine, only cyclic ancestry
           errors."""]]

    [child id=include-depth n=2.9.3 title='Depth limit'
      [body
        """Default maximum include nesting depth is 8. Exceeding it is
           refused with `cx-err:E905`, naming the limit and the path
           that crossed it. The bound is configurable by the embedding
           (the resolver options), not by environment variable."""]]

    [child id=include-capabilities n=2.9.4 title='Capability gating'
      [body
        """An include is a filesystem read, and reads are capabilities:
           `[?cx include=…]` refuses with `CXER0271` unless the run was
           granted `--allow-read` — deny-by-default, like every effect
           (see [[capabilities]]). Resolution is rooted: relative
           include paths resolve against the including document's
           directory, or against an explicit `--include-root=DIR`.
           Untrusted-input processing grants nothing and parses pure;
           see [[security]] for the threat model."""]]]

  [child id=namespaces n=2.10 title='Namespaces (xmlns:)'
    [intro
      """CX supports XML-namespace-style scoping via `xmlns:` attribute
         declarations. The normative spec is
         [`cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md) §3 (model)
         with the canonical-form rules in
         [`canonical.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/canonical.md) §2.7a."""]

    [child id=ns-prefix-decl n=2.10.1 title='Prefix declarations'
      [body
        """`xmlns:prefix=URI` declares a namespace binding scoped to the
           declaring element and all of its descendants until a
           redeclaration ends the scope. A prefix is then a `prefix:local`
           qualified name on any element or attribute in scope."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[doc xmlns:dc=http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/
           [dc:title 'CX Spec']
           [dc:author 'Ada Lovelace']]"""]]

    [child id=ns-default n=2.10.2 title='Default namespace'
      [body
        """`xmlns=URI` declares a default namespace. Applies to the
           declaring element and all unprefixed descendant **elements**
           until redeclared. Per XML Namespaces 1.0 §6.2 it does **not**
           apply to attribute names."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[doc xmlns=urn:doc
           [section 'Unprefixed elements live in urn:doc']]"""]]

    [child id=ns-reserved n=2.10.3 title='Reserved prefixes'
      [body
        """Three prefixes are reserved by CX and cannot be redeclared:"""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'prefix'] [th 'URI'] [th 'notes']]
        [tr [td 'xml'] [td 'http://www.w3.org/XML/1998/namespace'] [td 'XML built-in; always resolves; xml:lang accepted as alias for cx:lang']]
        [tr [td 'cx'] [td 'tag:cxhome.org,2026:ns/cx'] [td 'CX metadata; cannot be redeclared; carries cx:lang etc.']]
        [tr [td 'xmlns'] [td 'declaration syntax only'] [td 'Never resolves as a name prefix']]]]

    [child id=ns-equality n=2.10.4 title='Equality and canonical form'
      [body
        """Two namespace bindings are equal iff their URIs are equal as
           byte sequences (no normalization beyond percent-decoding).
           Canonical form rewrites prefixes to a deterministic sequence
           (`ns0`, `ns1`, …) so two documents with the same data but
           different prefix choices hash to the same canonical bytes.
           Round-trip preserves the original prefix names via the
           lossless canonicalizer."""]]]

  [child id=locale n=2.11 title='Locale and language (cx:lang)'
    [intro
      """`cx:lang` declares the language of an element's text content
         using a BCP 47 language tag. Inherited-scope: the declaration
         applies to the element and all descendants until a redeclaration.
         The reserved `cx:` prefix that carries it is pinned in
         [[ns-reserved]]."""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[doc cx:lang=en-US
         [title 'Welcome']
         [section cx:lang=fr-FR
           [title 'Bienvenue']]
         [section cx:lang=ja-JP
           [title 'ようこそ']]]"""]
    [body
      """The `xml:lang` attribute is accepted on input as an alias for
         `cx:lang` (per XML interop). On canonical emit, `cx:lang` is the
         normalized form. Every binding exposes a `.lang()` accessor on
         Nodes that resolves to the inherited language tag at that
         position."""]
    [body
      """Bidirectional text direction (LTR/RTL) is inferred from the
         language tag unless explicitly overridden with
         `cx:dir=rtl`/`cx:dir=ltr` (Unicode bidi algorithm)."""]]

  [child id=doctype n=2.12 title='Doctype declarations'
    [intro
      """CX reserves a doctype declaration slot — the `[!…]` declaration
         lexical space inherited from XML; an XML DOCTYPE imports as a
         `[!DOCTYPE …]` prolog node (see [[surfaces]]) — but does not yet
         define an active doctype surface."""]
    [body
      """The roadmap direction: a doctype names the document type
         (convention `<name>-v<major>`) and a future revision promotes
         it to an *active* declaration — auto-load the schema by name
         and validate on parse. Until that lands, associate a schema
         explicitly (see [[schema-binding]])."""]]

  [child id=encoding n=2.13 title='Document encoding (UTF-8, BOM, alternates)'
    [intro
      """CX source is UTF-8. The parser accepts a UTF-8 BOM at the
         start of a file (and strips it) but does not emit a BOM in
         canonical form."""]
    [body
      """Canonical-form output is always UTF-8 without BOM. Decoding
         errors raise a parse failure with a byte offset; the parser
         does not silently replace invalid bytes. Alternate source
         encodings (utf-16, legacy 8-bit code pages, CJK encodings)
         are on the roadmap and are not accepted today — transcode to
         UTF-8 before parsing."""]]

  [child id=streaming-data n=2.14 title='Streaming parse and emission'
    [intro
      """CX defines a SAX/StAX-style streaming event API — read events
         via `cx_events_open` / `next` / `close`, write events via the
         `cx_events_writer_*` family. Normative spec:
         [`spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md)."""]
    [body
      """The API contract is streaming — the consumer sees one event at
         a time and never needs the whole tree. The current engine
         behind it is **eager**: it parses the full document and then
         delivers the event list, so today the API bounds the
         *consumer's* memory, not the parser's. Line-oriented inputs
         can be processed with genuinely bounded residency by iterating
         lines and parsing each record separately:"""]
    [example lang=cx
      """[# Stream a large record-per-line log — one line resident
            at a time, never the whole file. #]
         [?lib 'cx-stdlib/io']
         [?lib 'cx-stdlib/cx']
         [?for [in $line [$io:line-iter [$io:open 'logs.cxl' 'r']]]
           [where [= [$name [$cx:parse $line]] 'error']]
           [yield [?let [= $rec [$cx:parse $line]] $rec/@code]]]"""]
    [body
      """Performance is tracked against the per-operation budgets in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md)
         §4 (parse and event-delivery targets per input size), enforced
         by the perf-regression suite rather than quoted as a single
         throughput figure."""]
    [body
      """For columnar tables specifically, the streaming format is CXCol
         (chunked column-oriented binary, see [[cxcol]]); the per-chunk
         manifest carries statistics enabling predicate pushdown."""]]

  [child id=limits n=2.15 title='Limits and quotas'
    [intro
      """The parse surface carries a small set of **structural guards**
         against adversarial input. They are deliberately few, they are
         built in rather than tuned by environment variables, and each
         refusal names its limit."""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'guard'] [th 'bound'] [th 'refusal']]
      [tr [td 'Element nesting (text parser)'] [td '64'] [td 'parse refusal naming the line, column, and limit']]
      [tr [td 'Include depth'] [td '8 (embedding-configurable)'] [td [code 'cx-err:E905']]]
      [tr [td 'Include cycle'] [td 'refused at the repeat visit'] [td [code 'cx-err:E904'] ' naming the chain']]
      [tr [td 'Include escape — absolute path / root traversal / URL scheme'] [td 'refused outright'] [td [code 'cx-err:E901'] ' / ' [code 'cx-err:E902'] ' / ' [code 'cx-err:E903']]]
      [tr [td 'CXCol decode recursion'] [td '64'] [td 'decode refusal naming the limit']]]
    [body
      """Two classes of bomb are **structurally absent** rather than
         capped. Entity references parse to nodes — there is no textual
         expansion pass — so recursive expansion attacks (the XML
         "billion laughs") have nothing to attack. And includes cannot
         fan out beyond the root-bounded tree above: no search paths, no
         URLs, no absolute paths."""]
    [body
      """What the engine deliberately does **not** carry: attribute-count
         caps, body-size caps, parse-time budgets, or canonical-form
         size caps. Parse cost is linear in input bytes, so the
         untrusted-input posture is boundary-side: bound the input size
         where it enters, run with the empty grant set (the parser needs
         no capability grants at all), and pair with OS-level process
         limits. See [[security]] for the threat model. Evaluation-side
         bounds (recursion, time) are the code language's story, not the
         data format's — Ring 0 cannot execute anything."""]]

  [child id=datetime-detail n=2.16 title='Date / time semantics — timezones, precision, leap seconds'
    [intro
      """Dates and datetimes are first-class scalars (see [[dates]]).
         This section is the developer-facing detail."""]
    [body
      """**Calendar:** proleptic Gregorian only. Dates before 1582-10-15
         are accepted but represent calendar-extension (the calendar in
         use today extended backward), not historical Julian dates."""]
    [body
      """**Datetime form:** `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS[.fff[fff[fff]]][offset]`.
         Optional fractional seconds at millisecond / microsecond /
         nanosecond precision. Offset is `Z` (UTC) or `±HH:MM`. CX accepts
         both ISO 8601 and RFC 3339 forms on input; canonical output is
         RFC 3339 with offset `Z` for UTC or `±HH:MM` otherwise."""]
    [body
      """**Timezone handling:** the lossless form (`cx fmt`) preserves
         the offset as authored; the **strict canonical form normalizes
         to UTC** — `2026-05-22T10:00:00-04:00` canonicalizes (and
         hashes) as `2026-05-22T14:00:00Z`. Identity comparison `=`
         compares the absolute instant, and sort order is by absolute
         instant."""]
    [body
      """**Leap seconds:** CX datetimes do not represent leap seconds
         (no `23:59:60`). Input containing `23:59:60` is normalized to
         the following `00:00:00`. Bindings that need leap-second
         awareness use an external library."""]
    [body
      """**Host language mapping:** Python `datetime` (aware, with
         tzinfo); Go `time.Time`; Rust `chrono::DateTime<FixedOffset>`;
         V `time.Time`. Each binding's Layer-1 surface preserves the
         offset; conversion to UTC is opt-in via `.to_utc()`."""]]

  [child id=reserved-tokens n=2.17 title='Reserved tokens and keyword list'
    [intro
      """A complete enumeration of identifiers that have special meaning
         and cannot be used as bare element names or attribute keys
         without quoting."""]

    [body
      """**Reserved literal tokens** (always have their literal meaning):"""]
    [list
      [item """`true`, `false` — boolean literals"""]
      [item """`null` — null scalar"""]]

    [body
      """**Type names** (after the glued `::` ascription, per the
         lexicon's TypeName production):"""]
    [list
      [item """`int`, `i8`, `i16`, `i32`, `i64`"""]
      [item """`u8`, `u16`, `u32`, `u64`"""]
      [item """`float`, `f16`, `f32`, `f64`"""]
      [item """`bool`, `string`, `null`, `date`, `datetime`, `bytes`, `atom`"""]
      [item """`decimal`, `bigint` — exact numeric refinements"""]
      [item """`duration`, `period`, `instant` — temporal refinements"""]
      [item """`secret` — declassification-gated values"""]]

    [body
      """**Directive name keywords** (the first token after `[?`):"""]
    [body
      """All 80 directives in the current registry — see
         [[directives]] in the code section. Notable: `for`, `match`,
         `if`, `let`, `fn`, `def`, `try`, `modify`, `cx`, `pipe`."""]

    [body
      """**CXPath axis keywords** (before `::` inside path expressions):"""]
    [list
      [item """`child`, `descendant`, `descendant-or-self`"""]
      [item """`parent`, `ancestor`, `ancestor-or-self`"""]
      [item """`self`, `attribute`"""]
      [item """`following`, `preceding`, `following-sibling`, `preceding-sibling`"""]]

    [body
      """**XPath/CXPath builtin function names** — see [[builtins]] for
         the full catalog (~80 names). Notable: `count`, `name`,
         `local-name`, `position`, `last`, `string`, `number`,
         `boolean`, `not`, `exists`, `empty`, `concat`,
         `string-length`, `contains`."""]

    [body
      """**Reserved namespace prefixes:** `xml`, `cx`, `xmlns` (see
         [[ns-reserved]])."""]

    [body
      """If you need an element or attribute named after a reserved
         token, quote it: `['true' value=1]`, `[user 'true'=yes]`. The
         single-quoted form is the canonical escape; double-quoted also
         works on input."""]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/concepts/{data-model, sigils, types}.cx
     during the Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24).
     Concept-page framing on the data model, sigils, and typed
     values that complements the per-feature reference above. #]

  [child id=concepts-fold n=2.18 title='Concept framing — data model, sigils, types'
    [intro
      """The reference above describes each feature in isolation;
         the concept framing below shows how they fit together —
         the one tree shape behind six surfaces, the five sigils as
         structural reach, the auto-typing rules that pick a type
         from a lexical shape."""]

    [child id=cxdm-overview n=2.18.1 title='One tree, six surfaces'
      [body
        """CXDM is the shape every CX parse produces and every emit
           walks. Whether the source is CX, XML, JSON, YAML, TOML,
           or Markdown, the parser yields a tree in this single
           shape; whether the target is any of those six dialects,
           the emitter walks the same tree back out. Downstream
           code never branches on input format because the data
           model has already absorbed the difference."""]
      [body
        """A CXDM node is an Element with a name, an ordered list
           of attributes, an ordered list of body items, and a few
           optional structural slots — anchor, merge target,
           declared id, data type. Body items are Text nodes,
           Scalar nodes, child Elements, or the collection literals
           — Sequences, Arrays, Maps."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[pizza #margherita :large size=14 crust=thin
             [topping cheese]
             [topping basil]
             Stone-baked since 1987.]"""]
      [body
        """Reading this element from the outside in: name `pizza`,
           declared id `margherita`, data-type annotation `:large`,
           attributes `size=14` and `crust=thin`, then a body of
           three items — two child elements and a text node."""]
      [note
        """Whitespace inside a body is significant when it sits
           between text characters and collapses to a single space
           when it separates body items. The canonical form
           normalises this so byte-identical content carries the
           same hash regardless of source author choice."""]]

    [child id=sigils-catalog n=2.18.2 title='The five sigils + bool sugar'
      [body
        """CX uses five sigils. Each is a single character in a
           fixed lexical position that gives a value structural
           meaning beyond what bare text can carry."""]
      [list
        [item """`@` — reference in attribute-value position to a
                declared id (`#`); in body position the form is
                `[ref @name]`. Never references anchors."""]
        [item """`&` — anchor declaration; names an element so
                others can refer to or merge from it."""]
        [item """`*` — merge target; folds the named anchor's attrs
                and children into this element."""]
        [item """`#` — declared id; stable handle within the
                document's scope; target of `@` references."""]
        [item """`:` — data type annotation (glued `::T`) and atom
                literal (`:name`); the ascription pins the value
                type, overriding auto-typing."""]]
      [example lang=cx
        """[menu
             [pizza #margherita price=12]
             [pizza #pepperoni  price=14]]
           [order #o1234 customer=alice
             [line item=@margherita count=2]  [; @-sigil reference to a declared id ]
             [line item=@pepperoni  count=1]]"""]
      [body
        """In CXPath the same `@` sigil is attribute access:"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[order [line sku='margherita' count=2]]
           [?for [in $line //order/line] [yield
             [row sku=$line/@sku]]]            [; @-sigil attribute access in CXPath ]"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3]
           [server *shared name=api]
           [server *shared name=worker]"""]
      [note
        """The strict canonical form expands anchors and removes the
           anchor structure entirely, so two documents that bind the
           same data with different anchor names hash to the same
           identity — no anchor name survives into the canonical
           bytes."""]
      [body
        """Boolean attributes have no shorthand sigil — they are
           ordinary `name=value` pairs (see [[bool]])."""]
      [example lang=cx """[pizza vegan=true nuts=false gluten=false organic=true]"""]]

    [child id=types-fold n=2.18.3 title='Types — auto-typing, sized, decimal, bigint'
      [body
        """CX values fall into a small set of atomic types — `int`,
           `float`, `bool`, `string`, `null`, `date`, `datetime`,
           `bytes`, `decimal`, `bigint`, `atom` — and
           arrays of any of these. Most values are auto-typed from
           their lexical shape; explicit annotations cover the
           rest."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[demo
             count=42                      # int
             pi=3.14                       # float
             enabled=true                  # bool
             name=Alice                    # string
             missing=null                  # null
             date=2026-05-09               # date
             start=2026-05-09T10:00:00Z]   # datetime"""]
      [note
        """**Leading-zero strings.** Tokens with a leading zero
           stay strings — `0x` (hex) is the **only** numeric radix
           prefix; there is no octal or binary literal form. Postal
           codes, area codes, account numbers, BIC codes — anywhere
           the leading zero is data, not a numeric value — round-trip
           as the strings they are."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[zip 02134]               # string '02134', not int 2134
           [mask 0xFF00FF]           # int 16711935 (hex)
           [perms 0o755]             # string '0o755' — no octal literals
           [bits 0b1101]             # string '0b1101' — no binary literals"""]
      [body
        """**Sized integer and float.** A colon-type pins the
           runtime representation and (for sized variants) the
           legal range. Out-of-range values fail at parse time,
           not at runtime in some downstream consumer."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[port::u16 8080]              # rejects values over 65535
           [balance::i64 -1234567890]    # signed 64-bit
           [ratio::f32 3.14]             # 32-bit float
           [count::u8 200]               # rejects 256 and up"""]
      [body
        """**Decimal and bigint.** IEEE 754 floats lose precision
           for money and high-precision quantities; signed 64-bit
           overflows for some IDs. CX has `:decimal` and `:bigint`
           for both cases, with binding mappings to each language's
           native big-number type."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[total::decimal 19.99]
           [user-id::bigint 18446744073709551616]"""]
      [body
        """**Typed bodies and arrays.** A glued `::T` ascription
           pins not just attribute values but body content as
           well."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[count::int 42]
           [ratio::decimal 3.14159]
           [oven-temps::int[] 220 240 260 480]
           [tags::[] cheap fast tasty]"""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/reference/{schemas,includes,streaming,tables}.cx
     during Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24). Compact
     quick-references that complement the per-feature reference
     above and link out to the normative spec docs. #]

  [child id=reference-quickref n=2.19 title='Reference quick-refs — schemas, includes, streaming, tables'
    [intro
      """At-a-glance references for the four big data-language
         subsystems that get their own normative spec docs.
         Schemas validate shape; includes compose documents;
         streaming bounds RSS; tables carry columnar data. The
         primary children (§2.7, §2.8, §2.9, §2.14) carry the
         per-feature depth; this section is the cookbook-style
         summary."""]

    [child id=schema-quickref n=2.19.1 title='Schemas — validate before processing'
      [body
        """A schema in CX is itself a CX document. It declares
           which elements may appear where, what attributes they
           carry, and what types those attributes hold. The `cx
           validate` subcommand walks the schema against an input
           document and reports diagnostics. Three modes
           determine what happens to undeclared content: **open**
           (the default) admits it silently; **strict** reports it
           as warnings; **closed** rejects it as errors. The schema
           sets its own mode in the header; the `--mode` flag
           overrides it."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[schema of=pizza]
           [pizza
             [attr size::[enum small medium large]]
             [attr crust::string]]"""]
      [example lang=shell
        """cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs
           cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs --mode=strict
           cx validate order.cx --schema=order.cxs --fail-on=warn"""]
      [note
        """**Type catalog.** `string`; `int` with sized variants
           (`i8`, `u16`, `i64`, …); `float`; `decimal`; `bigint`;
           `bool`; `date`; `datetime`; `bytes`; `atom`; plus the
           composite `[enum V…]` / `[or T…]` / `[list T]` forms. A
           regex requirement is not a type — it is the `[pattern S]`
           constraint clause on a string declaration. The validator
           coerces and bounds-checks each value against the
           declared type."""]]

    [child id=include-quickref n=2.19.2 title='Includes — parse-time vs eval-time'
      [body
        """`[?cx include=path]` is the include directive — a
           **parse-time** expansion that splices files before the
           document reaches downstream tools. Use it for static
           composition: config files, schema fragments, page
           templates with known paths. Resolution is opt-in — the
           directive is preserved untouched unless a resolution root
           is supplied (`--include-root=DIR` on the CLI). A program
           that needs a *computed* path reads the file itself with
           the standard library's `[$io:read-file]` + `[$cx:parse]`."""]
      [example lang=shell """cx --cx --include-root=./conf main.cx"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[users
             [?cx include=lib/admins.cx]
             [user name=carol]]"""]
      [note
        """**Unresolved includes and hashing.** Without a resolution
           root the directive rides along unresolved — and strict
           canonicalization strips directives, so the include
           **silently disappears** from `cx canonical` / `cx hash`
           output. Always resolve (supply `--include-root`) before
           canonicalizing or hashing a document that uses includes."""]
      [note
        """**Security and cycle detection.** Resolution rejects
           absolute paths (`E901`), paths that escape the root
           after lexical normalisation (`E902`), and URL-shaped
           paths (`E903`). Cycle detection (`E904`) and depth
           limits (`E905`, default 8) protect against runaway
           recursion. The error catalog lives in
           [`spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md)."""]]

    [child id=streaming-quickref n=2.19.3 title='Streaming — event shape and throughput'
      [body
        """The streaming API is a SAX/StAX-style event sequence
           ([`spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/streaming.md)).
           The read side is lossless: **32 event types** in six
           groups — lifecycle (`StartDoc`/`EndDoc`), prolog and
           markup, element + body (`StartElement` carries the name
           AND all attributes; there is no separate attribute
           event, and text arrives as whole `Text` / `Scalar`
           events, not chunks), collection literals and
           first-class kinds, the programmable surface, and the
           chunked-table triplet. The write side accepts a
           **14-event subset** and rejects the rest at emit time.
           The same event shape works on every input format."""]
      [body
        """Today the engine behind the read side is **eager** — the
           document is parsed whole and the events are delivered
           from the completed parse. The event contract is what
           consumers program against; bounded-memory parsing is the
           contract's headroom, not yet its implementation."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cx
           # cx.stream takes the document TEXT, not a file object.
           text = open('events.cx').read()
           for event in cx.stream(text):
               if event.kind == 'StartElement' and event.name == 'error':
                   print(event.attrs)

           # Write side: the C ABI exposes the cx_events_writer_*
           # family (14 emit events x 6 output formats)."""]
      [note
        """**Backpressure.** The write side has no buffering knobs —
           emitted bytes flow to the destination and the only
           backpressure is the operating system's own `write(2)`
           blocking. Performance is tracked against the budgets in
           [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md)
           §4."""]]

    [child id=tables-quickref n=2.19.4 title='Tables and binary forms'
      [body
        """A CX table is a structural form for columnar data.
           Columns declare a name and a type; rows are records.
           The Table API exposes count, schema introspection,
           dump, and load — symmetric inverses with declared
           types preserved across formats. The on-disk forms are
           **ast-bin** (binary AST, cap bit 36 advertises v8) and
           **data-bin** (CXCol v1 — columnar, used by
           Arrow/Parquet bridges)."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[inventory [table[size count::int]]
             large 12
             small 8
           ]"""]
      [example lang=shell
        """cx table info orders.cx      # column / row counts, types, byte size
           cx table dump orders.cx --to=cx
           cx table dump orders.cx --to=parquet --output=orders.parquet
           cx table load orders.parquet --to=cx
           # CSV enters through the convert surface, not `cx table load`:
           cx --from=csv --to=cx orders.csv"""]]]
]
